diagnosis_text:
No network fault exists. The topology uses RIP (Routing Information Protocol) as its dynamic routing protocol across three routers — not OSPF — and all three routers (router0, router1, router2) are fully operational with complete, FIB-installed routing tables. The ospf_snapshot's flags about missing ospfd are an expected and harmless artefact of a non-OSPF topology; zebra is running on all routers and RIP is distributing routes correctly.

The user's claim that "everything past our gateway is dead" is directly refuted by live traffic evidence: office_pc (192.168.3.2) achieved 0% packet loss to both pc0 (192.168.4.2, Principle LAN, two hops via router1) and pc5 (128.168.0.5, Internet LAN, three hops via router1→router2) at the time of diagnosis. router1 — the router "in the middle" the user suspects has died — is alive, holds connected routes for 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24, and correctly forwards traffic onward via router2.

All infrastructure layers were inspected: no ACL drop rules, no ARP or MAC conflicts, no incorrect addressing, no traffic shaping, no resource pressure, no crashed processes. The network is healthy end-to-end. The corrective action is none — the reported outage is unconfirmed by any live evidence and the network requires no intervention.
=== END ===